QB.push(
{i:126,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"GEN",diff:1,
q:"In the Claude Messages API, which field on the response tells your agent loop whether Claude is requesting a tool call or has finished its turn?",
ask:"Which field carries this signal?",
a:["<code>stop_reason</code>",
"<code>role</code>",
"<code>usage.output_tokens</code>",
"<code>content[0].type</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>stop_reason</code> is the structural control signal: <code>tool_use</code>, <code>end_turn</code>, <code>max_tokens</code>, <code>stop_sequence</code> and others.",
"<code>role</code> is always <code>assistant</code> on a response; it carries no control information.",
"Token counts are for billing and budgeting, not flow control.",
"Inspecting content blocks can tell you a <code>tool_use</code> block exists, but <code>stop_reason</code> is the authoritative field and is what the loop should branch on."],
e:"A direct recall item of the kind the exam mixes in among the scenarios. Your loop reads <code>stop_reason</code> and branches: <code>tool_use</code> means execute the requested tools and continue; <code>end_turn</code> means Claude finished naturally and the loop stops; <code>max_tokens</code> means the output was truncated, so any partial tool call must be discarded rather than executed; <code>stop_sequence</code> means a configured stop sequence was hit.",
k:"<code>stop_reason</code> drives the agent loop. Know all its values, not just the two common ones."},

{i:127,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"GEN",diff:2,
q:"A <code>tool_result</code> content block must reference the tool call it answers.",
ask:"Which field provides that link?",
a:["<code>tool_use_id</code>, matching the <code>id</code> of the corresponding <code>tool_use</code> block",
"<code>name</code>, matching the tool's name",
"<code>index</code>, matching the position of the tool call in the content array",
"<code>request_id</code>, taken from the API response headers"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Every <code>tool_use</code> block carries an <code>id</code>, and the answering <code>tool_result</code> must set <code>tool_use_id</code> to that value.",
"The tool name is insufficient &mdash; the same tool can be called twice in one turn with different arguments.",
"Position is not the pairing mechanism, and relying on it breaks when results return out of order.",
"<code>request_id</code> identifies the HTTP request for support purposes, not an individual tool call."],
e:"Pairing by <code>tool_use_id</code> is what makes parallel tool use work: several calls in one assistant turn, several results in one user turn, each unambiguously matched. Two rules follow. Every <code>tool_use</code> block must receive a matching <code>tool_result</code> in the immediately following user message, or the API rejects the request. And because pairing is by id rather than position, the execution order of independent tools is entirely your choice.",
k:"<code>tool_result.tool_use_id</code> must match <code>tool_use.id</code>. Every tool call needs a result in the very next user message."},

{i:128,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"GEN",diff:2,
q:"How do you signal to Claude that a tool execution failed, using the Messages API?",
ask:"Which is correct?",
a:["Return a <code>tool_result</code> block with <code>is_error: true</code> and a message describing the failure",
"Omit the <code>tool_result</code> block for the failed call",
"Return an HTTP 500 from your application to the API",
"Set <code>stop_reason</code> to <code>error</code> in your next request"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The failure is communicated in-band as a <code>tool_result</code> the model can reason about and recover from.",
"Omitting a result leaves a <code>tool_use</code> block unanswered, which the API rejects.",
"Your application is the client, not the server; it does not return HTTP statuses to the API.",
"<code>stop_reason</code> is set by the API on responses; clients do not send it on requests."],
e:"Tool failures are ordinary data in the conversation, not transport errors. Return the <code>tool_result</code> with <code>is_error: true</code> and a <em>specific</em> message &mdash; what was attempted, why it failed, and whether retrying could help. That specificity is what lets Claude choose between retrying, taking an alternative route, and escalating. The recurring anti-pattern is a generic <code>\"Error\"</code> string, which collapses transient, validation, permission and business-rule failures into one indistinguishable signal.",
k:"Tool failure &rarr; <code>tool_result</code> with <code>is_error: true</code> + a specific, actionable message. Never omit the result, never return a bare &ldquo;Error&rdquo;."},

{i:129,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CAP",diff:3,
q:"A conversational agent handles a 60-turn support session. Around turn 45 it begins contradicting a constraint the customer stated at turn 3 (&ldquo;I'm on the legacy plan, the new features don't apply to me&rdquo;), despite the full history being sent every request.",
ask:"Which remedy is most appropriate?",
a:["Extract durable case facts into a persistent block that is re-injected on every request, outside the summarised conversational history",
"Send the conversation history twice so the early turns appear again",
"Ask the customer to restate their plan type periodically",
"Reduce the temperature so the model contradicts itself less"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A compact, stable facts block keeps load-bearing details salient regardless of how long the conversation grows.",
"Duplicating history doubles the cost and worsens the middle-of-context problem rather than fixing it.",
"Shifts the system's job onto the customer and produces a visibly incompetent experience.",
"Temperature affects sampling variance, not the salience of a fact buried 42 turns back."],
e:"This is the lost-in-the-middle effect in a conversational setting: models process the beginning and end of long inputs reliably and are less dependable about the middle. The prescribed remedy is a <em>case facts</em> block &mdash; plan type, account status, verified identity, confirmed constraints, decisions already made &mdash; maintained by your code and re-injected in a stable, privileged position on every request. It is small, cheap, and immune to being scrolled into the middle.",
k:"Lost in the middle: models attend reliably to the start and end. Keep durable facts in a small persistent block, not buried in history."},

{i:130,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your agent uses seven tools. You add eleven more from a newly connected MCP server. Tool-selection accuracy drops noticeably even for the original seven.",
ask:"What is the most likely explanation and fix?",
a:["A larger tool surface degrades selection reliability; scope the agent to role-relevant tools and delegate the rest to specialised agents",
"The new MCP server's tools are broken and should be reported upstream",
"The system prompt is now too short relative to the tool definitions",
"Tool definitions are being truncated, so some are invisible to the model"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Selection reliability falls as the toolset grows &mdash; the blueprint contrasts 18 tools with a focused 4&ndash;5.",
"The original seven also degraded, so this is about the choice space, not about any individual tool being faulty.",
"System prompt length has no such relationship to tool count.",
"Tool definitions are not silently truncated; they are sent in full or the request fails."],
e:"More tools means more opportunities for descriptions to overlap and for the model to pick a near-neighbour. The remedies, in order: scope each agent to role-relevant tools plus a few cross-role utilities; sharpen descriptions so each names its trigger conditions and its boundaries against similar tools; and where a broad capability is genuinely needed occasionally, delegate to a specialised subagent that owns it rather than adding it to everyone's list.",
k:"Tool count degrades selection. Target a small role-relevant set (roughly 4&ndash;5); delegate the rest to specialised agents."},

{i:131,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent successfully resolves an issue but the conversation runs to 22 tool-calling iterations. Reviewing the trace, 9 of those calls fetch data the agent had already retrieved earlier in the same conversation.",
ask:"Which fix addresses the root cause?",
a:["Return results in a clearly labelled, structured form and keep a compact working-state summary so the agent can see what it already has",
"Cache tool results transparently so repeat calls are fast",
"Reduce the iteration cap so the agent is forced to be efficient",
"Add an instruction telling the agent not to call the same tool twice"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The agent re-fetches because it cannot readily see what it already holds; making current state legible removes the need.",
"A worthwhile optimisation for latency and cost, but the agent still burns a turn on each redundant call &mdash; and turns, not tool latency, dominate.",
"Caps truncate the symptom and turn thorough conversations into abandoned ones.",
"A blunt instruction that will also suppress legitimate re-fetches after state changes."],
e:"Repeated retrieval is a legibility problem. Two complementary fixes: make each tool result structured and labelled so it is easy to recognise later, and maintain a compact working-state block (verified customer, order in scope, facts established) that is re-injected each turn. Caching is still worth doing for cost and latency, but it addresses a different axis &mdash; it makes the wasted call cheap rather than unnecessary.",
k:"Redundant tool calls = the agent cannot see what it already has. Fix with labelled results + a compact working-state block, not with caps."},

{i:132,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"You are asked whether the coordinator should perform synthesis itself, since it already holds every subagent's results.",
ask:"What is the strongest reason to keep synthesis as a separate agent?",
a:["Synthesis needs its own focused system prompt and quality criteria, and doing it in the coordinator mixes orchestration state with authoring work in one context",
"Coordinators are technically prohibited from producing final output",
"Synthesis always requires tools the coordinator cannot have",
"A separate agent is always cheaper in tokens"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Specialisation and context hygiene: the coordinator's context is full of orchestration bookkeeping that is noise for an authoring task.",
"No such prohibition exists &mdash; for a small task, coordinator-authored output is perfectly reasonable.",
"Synthesis often needs no tools at all; this is not the distinguishing factor.",
"Usually false. A separate agent means re-passing the material, so it typically costs more."],
e:"Note the honesty required here: for a small, two-source task, having the coordinator write the summary is fine and cheaper. The separation earns its cost when synthesis is substantial &mdash; it deserves a dedicated system prompt encoding structure, tone, attribution rules and how to render conflicts, and it benefits from a clean context containing the findings rather than the orchestration trace. Recognising when <em>not</em> to add an agent is as heavily tested as recognising when to.",
k:"Separate synthesis when authoring is substantial enough to need its own prompt and a clean context. For trivial merges, the coordinator can do it."},

{i:133,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"A coordinator dispatches work to a subagent that fails with a permission error. The coordinator retries twice, then falls back to a different subagent that lacks the required capability and produces a poor result, which is delivered without comment.",
ask:"What are the two defects?",
a:["A permission error was treated as retryable, and the degraded fallback result was delivered without disclosing the degradation",
"The coordinator should have aborted the entire workflow on the first failure",
"The subagent should have suppressed the permission error and returned an empty result",
"The coordinator retried too few times before falling back"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Retry policy ignored the error category, and the coverage annotation that would have made the degradation visible was missing.",
"Aborting on a single failure is the opposite anti-pattern &mdash; it discards recoverable situations.",
"Suppressing an error and returning empty success is the most dangerous option of all.",
"More retries of a deterministic permission failure is more guaranteed waste."],
e:"Two Domain 1 principles are violated at once. First, retry policy follows error category: permission errors are not transient, so retrying is pure waste and the correct response is to obtain access, route differently, or escalate. Second, when the system degrades &mdash; a fallback with lesser capability &mdash; the output must say so. Coverage annotation turns an invisible quality drop into an informed one, so the reader knows where to be sceptical.",
k:"Never retry non-transient errors. When you degrade to a fallback, annotate the degradation in the output."},

{i:134,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"You want to reduce the chance that two parallel research subagents return the same sources.",
ask:"Which measure is most effective?",
a:["Give each subagent an explicitly disjoint scope including a statement of what the other agents are covering",
"Ask each subagent to avoid sources that seem obvious",
"Run them with different temperatures",
"Deduplicate at synthesis and accept the duplicated work"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Naming both the inclusion and the exclusion &mdash; and what the siblings own &mdash; is what makes disjointness actionable.",
"&ldquo;Obvious&rdquo; is undefined and subjective; both agents will apply it differently and still converge.",
"Temperature affects phrasing and sampling, not which sub-domain an agent searches.",
"Cleans up after paying full cost, and does nothing about the gaps that overlap usually accompanies."],
e:"Subagents run in isolation and therefore converge on the same prominent sources unless told otherwise. Explicit exclusions are markedly more effective than inclusions alone, because &ldquo;cover music&rdquo; still leaves the boundary with adjacent domains ambiguous, while &ldquo;cover music and audio production; visual art is covered by another agent, do not include it&rdquo; does not. Pair partitioning with a coverage check at synthesis to catch anything the partition itself missed.",
k:"State inclusions <em>and</em> exclusions, and tell each subagent what its siblings own. Isolated agents converge without explicit boundaries."},

{i:135,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your coordinator uses a general-purpose prompt to pick among five specialists. Routing accuracy is 71%. The five specialists' <code>description</code> fields are one line each and use overlapping vocabulary.",
ask:"What should you do first?",
a:["Rewrite the five descriptions to state trigger conditions, boundaries, and how each differs from the nearest alternative",
"Add a dedicated classifier model in front of the coordinator",
"Merge the five specialists into two broader agents",
"Provide the coordinator with 50 few-shot routing examples"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Descriptions are the primary selection signal, and overlapping vocabulary is the stated cause &mdash; this is the lowest-effort, highest-impact fix.",
"Adding a model to compensate for bad metadata is the over-engineering distractor. It also adds latency and a second thing to maintain.",
"Reduces the choice space by discarding specialisation, and broader agents bring back the large-toolset problem.",
"Examples help, but 50 of them is a lot of tokens spent working around descriptions you could simply fix. Try the root cause first."],
e:"This mirrors the published sample question about overlapping tool descriptions, applied to agent selection. The exam's stated reasoning is worth memorising: descriptions are the model's primary selection mechanism, so expanding them with input formats, example triggers, edge cases and applicability boundaries is the first move. A routing layer is over-engineering, merging is more effort than justified, and few-shot adds tokens without addressing the root cause.",
k:"Bad routing &rarr; fix the descriptions first. State triggers, boundaries and contrast with the nearest alternative. Classifiers are over-engineering."},

{i:136,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A multi-agent system produces good results but nobody can explain why a particular decision was made on a given run.",
ask:"Which architectural property most improves this?",
a:["Routing all inter-agent communication through the coordinator so a single trace records every delegation, result and routing decision",
"Increasing each agent's verbosity so it explains itself in prose",
"Asking each agent to output a confidence score with its result",
"Reducing the number of agents so there is less to trace"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A single choke point makes the whole run observable, which is one of the main reasons hub-and-spoke is preferred.",
"Prose self-explanation is unstructured, unreliable and inflates context without producing a queryable record.",
"Confidence scores are uncalibrated and, in any case, do not record what happened.",
"Fewer agents means less capability, not better observability &mdash; and two agents talking directly are just as opaque."],
e:"Observability is an architectural property, not a logging afterthought. Hub-and-spoke gives you one place where every delegation, every returned result and every routing decision passes, which makes the run reconstructable. Capture per delegation: which agent, the full prompt sent, the result returned, errors and their categories, latency and tokens. That trace is what turns &ldquo;the report missed music&rdquo; into &ldquo;the coordinator created three visual-domain subtasks&rdquo;.",
k:"Observability comes from topology. One hub = one trace. Record every delegation, prompt, result, error and routing decision."},

{i:137,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You are writing the delegation prompt for a subagent that must analyse three specific files and return findings in a fixed shape.",
ask:"Which element is most often missing and most damaging when absent?",
a:["The explicit output contract stating exactly what fields to return and what to do when information is unavailable",
"A polite greeting establishing tone",
"The name of the coordinator that dispatched it",
"The total number of subagents running in parallel"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Without a stated contract the subagent improvises a shape, and the coordinator's aggregation breaks or silently misreads it.",
"Tone has no bearing on the reliability of a delegated analysis.",
"The subagent does not need to know who dispatched it.",
"Irrelevant to the subagent, which cannot see or coordinate with its siblings anyway."],
e:"A complete delegation has four parts: the goal, the full inputs, the output contract, and the quality criteria. The output contract is the one most often skipped and the most damaging to skip, because its absence fails silently &mdash; you get a plausible answer in an unexpected shape. Specify the fields, whether each is required, and the explicit representation for missing information (<code>null</code> or <code>\"not_found\"</code>) so the subagent reports absence instead of inventing a value.",
k:"Delegation = goal + full inputs + output contract + quality criteria. Always define how to represent missing information."},

{i:138,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"DPT",diff:3,
q:"A subagent is asked to find every caller of a function. It returns four call sites. A human later finds eleven, because seven go through a wrapper module.",
ask:"What should the delegation have specified?",
a:["That indirect callers through wrappers and re-exports must be traced, with the method for doing so",
"That the subagent should have used a larger model",
"That the subagent should search faster",
"That the results should be returned as JSON"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The requirement to trace transitively was implicit; making it explicit is what closes the gap.",
"A larger model still answers the question it was asked, and it was asked for direct callers.",
"Speed is unrelated to completeness.",
"Format matters for parsing, not for whether the search was exhaustive."],
e:"&ldquo;Find every caller&rdquo; hides a definitional question: does a call through a wrapper count? The blueprint specifically mentions tracing function usage through wrapper modules as an expected skill. The general lesson is that completeness requirements must be spelled out &mdash; state that indirect and re-exported usages count, suggest the method (grep the symbol, then grep the wrapper's own exports), and require the subagent to state its coverage and any uncertainty rather than presenting four results as exhaustive.",
k:"Spell out completeness requirements. &ldquo;All callers&rdquo; must say whether indirect calls through wrappers and re-exports count."},

{i:139,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Which of the following is best enforced with prompt guidance rather than programmatic controls?",
ask:"Which belongs in the prompt?",
a:["Preferring to check the cheaper data source before the expensive one when both would answer the question",
"Blocking refunds above the approval threshold",
"Requiring identity verification before any account modification",
"Preventing writes to the production database"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A cost preference is an optimisation; violating it wastes a little money and nothing more, so soft guidance preserves useful flexibility.",
"A financial control with an audit consequence &mdash; needs a hook or precondition.",
"A security control gating account changes &mdash; needs a precondition in code.",
"An irreversible, high-blast-radius action &mdash; needs tool restriction or a permission rule."],
e:"The exam tests this from the prompt side too, so do not answer &ldquo;always code&rdquo; reflexively. Over-enforcement makes systems brittle and removes the adaptability that justifies using an agent: if the cheap source is down, you <em>want</em> the agent to use the expensive one. Reserve deterministic enforcement for consequences you cannot tolerate even once &mdash; money, security, compliance, irreversibility &mdash; and use prompts for preferences, priorities, style and judgement.",
k:"Prompts for preferences and judgement; code for consequences you cannot tolerate once. Over-enforcement is its own failure mode."},

{i:140,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"An agent handles a case where policy clearly applies and the action is within its authority, but the customer is extremely angry and has used strong language.",
ask:"Should the agent escalate?",
a:["No &mdash; resolve it, since anger is not a valid escalation trigger and the case is within scope and policy",
"Yes &mdash; strong emotion indicates the case needs human handling",
"Yes &mdash; escalate to protect the agent's resolution metrics",
"Only if a sentiment classifier scores the message above a hostility threshold"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The valid triggers are an explicit request for a human, a policy gap or exception, and inability to progress. Anger is none of them.",
"Sentiment is explicitly named as an unreliable proxy for case complexity.",
"Gaming a metric by handing off resolvable work is the failure the metric exists to detect.",
"Formalising an unreliable proxy with a threshold does not make it a valid trigger."],
e:"The blueprint is explicit that sentiment analysis and model self-rated confidence are unreliable proxies for case complexity. An angry customer with a straightforward in-policy return is best served by having it resolved immediately, not by being queued for a human. Tone should absolutely shape <em>how</em> the agent responds &mdash; acknowledge the frustration, be concise, act quickly &mdash; but it should not shape <em>whether</em> the agent resolves the case.",
k:"Escalation triggers: explicit request for a human, policy gap or exception, inability to progress. Not sentiment. Not self-rated confidence."},

{i:141,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You want a hook to add an audit annotation to every tool result without changing what the model sees semantically, and you must not break existing parsing.",
ask:"What is the main risk to manage?",
a:["Modifying result payloads can change how the model interprets them, so additions must be clearly separated and consistently structured",
"Hooks cannot modify tool results at all",
"Adding fields will exceed the tool's JSON Schema and cause validation failure",
"Audit data must never be visible to the model under any circumstances"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Anything you inject becomes part of the model's input and can shift interpretation, so it must be structurally distinct and consistent.",
"<code>PostToolUse</code> hooks exist precisely to modify results.",
"Tool <em>input</em> schemas constrain what the model sends. Result shape is not validated against them.",
"Too absolute &mdash; some metadata (source, timestamp, confidence) is genuinely useful to the model."],
e:"A <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that modifies results is powerful and easy to misuse. Two disciplines keep it safe: put injected metadata in a clearly separate, consistently named region rather than mixing it into the data fields, and apply the transformation uniformly so the model does not see the same tool return two different shapes. If the annotation is purely for your audit system and has no value to the model, emit it to the log rather than into the context, where it costs tokens and risks confusion.",
k:"Hook-injected metadata must be structurally separated and consistently applied. If the model does not need it, log it instead of injecting it."},

{i:142,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your compliance team asks for proof that the $500 refund limit was enforced on every refund last quarter.",
ask:"Which design makes this provable?",
a:["Enforce the limit in a hook or tool precondition that emits an audit record for every evaluation, including allows and blocks",
"Show the system prompt containing the policy statement",
"Sample 100 conversations and manually verify the limit was respected",
"Show that the model was instructed correctly and no complaints were received"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A deterministic control that logs every decision produces a complete, auditable record rather than an inference.",
"A prompt shows intent, not enforcement. It cannot demonstrate that the rule held in every case.",
"Sampling gives a statistical argument, not proof, and it is expensive to repeat every quarter.",
"Absence of complaints is not evidence of compliance; violations frequently go unreported."],
e:"Auditability is a design property, and it is a strong argument for code-level enforcement independent of the reliability argument. A control implemented as a hook or a tool precondition can log every evaluation &mdash; the amount, the decision, the rule version, the actor, the timestamp &mdash; producing a complete record. A prompt-based rule leaves nothing to audit but the prompt itself, and the only way to check compliance after the fact is to inspect conversations one at a time.",
k:"Code enforcement is auditable: log every evaluation, allows and blocks alike. Prompt-based rules leave nothing to audit but intent."},

{i:143,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"You need to add one field to an API response and update the two tests that assert on it. The change is fully understood.",
ask:"Which approach is appropriate?",
a:["Direct execution &mdash; make the change and run the tests",
"Planning mode, to explore alternatives first",
"A coordinator with subagents for the code change, the tests, and the documentation",
"Adaptive decomposition to discover what needs changing"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Simple, well-understood changes go straight to execution with verification.",
"Planning a fully understood one-field change is pure overhead.",
"Multi-agent orchestration for a three-line change is the over-engineering distractor.",
"There is nothing to discover; the change is already fully specified."],
e:"The exam deliberately includes cases where the sophisticated answer is wrong, because over-architecting is a real and costly failure mode. Planning mode, subagents and adaptive decomposition all carry overhead in latency, tokens and complexity, and they earn it only when the task has genuine uncertainty, scale or architectural consequence. A well-specified small change gets direct execution plus verification, and nothing more.",
k:"Match the machinery to the task. Small, well-understood changes get direct execution. Sophistication has a cost that must be earned."},

{i:144,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A long-running agent must survive process restarts and continue where it left off.",
ask:"Which two elements are essential?",
a:["Durable checkpointing of completed phases and their outputs, plus idempotent phase execution so an interrupted phase can safely re-run",
"A larger context window and a longer timeout",
"A retry loop and an iteration cap",
"Verbose logging and a monitoring dashboard"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Checkpointing tells you where to resume; idempotency makes resuming safe when a phase was interrupted mid-execution.",
"Neither survives a process restart &mdash; in-memory context is gone.",
"Retries and caps govern behaviour within a run, not recovery across runs.",
"Observability tells you what happened; it does not restore state or make re-execution safe."],
e:"Crash recovery needs both halves. Checkpointing alone leaves you unsure whether the phase you were in completed its side effects, so re-running it may duplicate work. Idempotency alone means you can safely re-run, but you have no idea where to start and re-run everything. Together: read the checkpoint, resume at the first incomplete phase, and rely on idempotency to make a partially-executed phase safe to repeat.",
k:"Crash recovery = durable checkpoints (where to resume) + idempotent phases (safe to re-run). Neither is sufficient alone."},

{i:145,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:3,
q:"Two developers resume the same named session simultaneously from different machines and both make edits.",
ask:"What is the underlying design problem?",
a:["Session state is being treated as shared mutable state without any coordination, so the two lines of work diverge and conflict",
"Named sessions are limited to one machine",
"Resuming a session locks it automatically, so one developer will simply be blocked",
"The second developer's session will silently inherit the first's edits"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Concurrent access to one session with no coordination protocol produces divergence and conflicting edits to the same files.",
"Portability is not the issue; the concurrency is.",
"Assumes a locking guarantee, and even if one existed, blocking a colleague is a workaround rather than a design.",
"Sessions do not merge or sync live; each resumed instance works from the state it loaded."],
e:"Sessions are personal working contexts, not a collaboration primitive. Where two people need to work on the same problem, the coordination belongs in the shared substrate they both already use &mdash; version control branches, an agreed division of the work, or a shared plan file that both sessions read and update. The general principle recurs throughout Domain 1: shared state without a coordination protocol produces divergence, whether the participants are people or agents.",
k:"Sessions are personal contexts, not shared state. Coordinate through version control and shared plan files, not by sharing a session."},

{i:146,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator receives one subagent result that is clearly low quality &mdash; three sentences with no sources &mdash; while the other three are thorough.",
ask:"What should it do?",
a:["Evaluate the result against its quality criteria and re-dispatch that subtask with specific feedback about what was missing",
"Pass all four results to synthesis and let it compensate",
"Discard the weak result and synthesise from the other three",
"Accept it, since the subagent reported success"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Detect the shortfall against explicit criteria, then re-dispatch with feedback specific enough to change the outcome.",
"Synthesis cannot manufacture evidence that was never gathered; the gap propagates into the report.",
"Silently dropping a subtopic leaves an unannotated hole &mdash; the reader cannot tell it is missing.",
"A success status describes completion, not quality. Quality is the coordinator's judgement to make."],
e:"Iterative refinement requires the coordinator to evaluate intermediate results rather than merely collect them. That needs explicit criteria (sources per claim, sub-domains covered, dates present) and specific feedback on re-dispatch &mdash; &ldquo;your response cited no sources and did not address streaming economics; return findings with URLs and publication dates&rdquo; produces a better second attempt than &ldquo;try again&rdquo;. If a second attempt also falls short, the honest outcome is to annotate the gap rather than hide it.",
k:"Coordinators evaluate results against criteria and re-dispatch with specific feedback. Success status &ne; acceptable quality."},

{i:147,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You need a subagent to have web access but not filesystem write access, and a second subagent to have the reverse.",
ask:"How is this expressed?",
a:["Per-agent tool lists in each <code>AgentDefinition</code>, granting only the tools that agent's role requires",
"A single global permission file applied to all agents equally",
"Instructions in each agent's system prompt describing what it may and may not use",
"A runtime check in each tool that inspects which agent is calling"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Capability is per-agent and is expressed by the tool list &mdash; the simplest and most robust mechanism.",
"A global policy cannot express different capabilities for different roles, which is the requirement.",
"System prompt instructions are guidance and confer no enforcement.",
"Workable as a defence-in-depth layer, but far more complex than simply not granting the tool in the first place."],
e:"Least privilege in the Agent SDK is expressed by what you put in each agent's tool list. Do not grant a tool and then try to fence it off &mdash; omit it. This also improves selection reliability as a side effect, since a smaller, role-relevant toolset is easier for the model to choose from correctly. Server-side checks in the tools themselves remain valuable for anything security-critical, but the primary mechanism is the grant.",
k:"Least privilege = per-agent tool lists. Omit the tool rather than granting it and restricting it by prompt."},

{i:148,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"A extraction agent must not process documents that fail a virus scan. The scan runs as a separate service.",
ask:"Where should this gate live?",
a:["In a precondition on the document-loading tool, which refuses to return content for an unscanned or failed document",
"In the agent's system prompt, instructing it to check scan status first",
"In a post-processing step that discards extractions from infected documents",
"In a nightly job that removes infected documents from the corpus"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Gate at the boundary where the content would enter the system, enforced by the tool that owns access to it.",
"An instruction the agent may or may not follow, guarding a security control.",
"Too late &mdash; the content has already been loaded and processed.",
"Leaves a window during which infected documents are processable, which is precisely the risk."],
e:"The general rule for security gates is to place them at the boundary the dangerous thing must cross, enforced by the component that owns that boundary. The loading tool is that component: it checks scan status and refuses to return content otherwise. Combine this with a clear non-retryable error explaining why, so the agent can report the situation coherently rather than retrying a load that will never succeed.",
k:"Place security gates at the crossing point, owned by the component that controls it. Return a clear non-retryable error on refusal."},

{i:149,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Which statement best captures the relationship between hooks and prompts for enforcing agent behaviour?",
ask:"Which is most accurate?",
a:["Hooks provide deterministic guarantees; prompts provide probabilistic compliance, and well-designed systems use both for different purposes",
"Hooks and prompts are interchangeable and you should pick whichever is easier",
"Prompts are preferred because they are easier to change than code",
"Hooks are preferred for all behaviour because prompts cannot be tested"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. This is the exam's central formulation, including the point that they are complementary rather than competing.",
"They differ fundamentally in what they guarantee; choosing by convenience is how compliance gaps appear.",
"Ease of change is real but irrelevant to whether a rule is guaranteed, and it argues for the weaker mechanism on a bad criterion.",
"Prompts absolutely can and should be tested with evaluation sets; and hooks are wrong for judgement-based behaviour."],
e:"Memorise the phrasing: hooks give deterministic guarantees, prompts give probabilistic compliance. The complementarity matters as much as the contrast &mdash; a hook that blocks a refund guarantees the outcome, and a prompt that teaches the policy makes the agent explain the block coherently and offer the escalation path proactively. Use hooks for hard rules and prompts for the judgement, tone and prioritisation that surrounds them.",
k:"Hooks = deterministic guarantee. Prompts = probabilistic compliance. Hard rules in hooks, judgement in prompts, both together."},

{i:150,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"A coordinator must research a topic where you cannot know in advance which sub-domains exist.",
ask:"What is the correct opening move?",
a:["Run a scoping pass to enumerate the sub-domains first, then partition and dispatch based on what that pass found",
"Guess three sub-domains and dispatch immediately to save time",
"Dispatch one subagent with the whole topic and let it decide",
"Dispatch ten subagents on overlapping slices to maximise the chance of coverage"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. When the space is unknown, discovering it is a distinct first step &mdash; this is what prevents the visual-art-only failure.",
"Guessing the partition is exactly how a topic gets silently narrowed to whatever came to mind first.",
"Forfeits parallelism and gives one agent an unbounded scope it will cover shallowly.",
"Overlapping slices duplicate heavily, and covering an unknown space by chance is not a strategy."],
e:"Adaptive decomposition begins with mapping. For research that means a cheap scoping pass that enumerates the dimensions of the topic before any deep work is dispatched; for a codebase it means mapping structure before reading files. The scoping output becomes the visible, inspectable partition &mdash; which is what makes coverage checkable later. It also gives the coordinator a basis for the second round of dispatch when the first round reveals something unexpected.",
k:"Unknown space &rarr; scope first, then partition and dispatch. The scoping output is what makes coverage inspectable."},

{i:151,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Your loop appends tool results correctly but sends only the last five messages plus the system prompt on each request, to control cost. The agent works well on short tasks and fails unpredictably on long ones.",
ask:"What is the correct characterisation?",
a:["A sliding window discards information by position rather than relevance, so long tasks lose facts established early",
"Five messages is too few and should be increased to twenty",
"The system prompt should also be truncated for consistency",
"Tool results should not be included in the window at all"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The flaw is the truncation policy itself, not its size &mdash; relevance and recency are different things.",
"Raising the number delays the failure to slightly longer tasks without changing its nature.",
"Truncating the system prompt removes the durable instructions, making things considerably worse.",
"Removing tool results breaks the loop's core contract; the model would never see what its tools returned."],
e:"Sliding windows are the classic cost optimisation that quietly breaks reliability. The durable alternatives: extract load-bearing facts into a persistent block re-injected every turn; trim verbose tool payloads by field rather than dropping whole messages; delegate verbose work to subagents so it never enters this context; and use prompt caching on the stable prefix to cut the cost of re-sending rather than cutting the content.",
k:"Never truncate by position. Extract facts to a persistent block, trim by field, delegate verbosity, and cache the stable prefix."},

{i:152,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An architect claims that because subagents have isolated context, the coordinator never needs to worry about context limits.",
ask:"Why is this wrong?",
a:["The coordinator accumulates every subagent's returned results plus its own orchestration state, so its context grows with the workflow",
"Coordinators have smaller context windows than subagents by design",
"Isolated contexts are shared with the coordinator after each subagent finishes",
"Coordinators cannot use context management techniques such as compaction"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Isolation protects the coordinator from subagents' <em>working</em> output, not from their returned results.",
"There is no such asymmetry; window size follows the model, not the role.",
"Nothing is shared automatically &mdash; only what the subagent explicitly returns.",
"Coordinators can compact, trim and externalise like any other context."],
e:"Isolation is a filter, not an exemption. The subagent's greps, dead ends and page reads stay in its own window; its returned conclusions land in the coordinator's. With enough subagents and verbose enough return payloads, the coordinator fills up regardless. That is why the two companion practices matter: scope each subagent's return contract to conclusions plus evidence, and persist completed phase results externally so only the working set stays live.",
k:"Isolation filters working output, not returned results. Scope return contracts and externalise completed phases to protect the coordinator."},

{i:153,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"A coordinator needs a subagent to check one fact and return quickly. It sends a 4,000-token prompt including the entire research brief.",
ask:"What is the problem?",
a:["The delegation is over-scoped for the task, wasting tokens and latency and inviting the subagent to do more than was asked",
"The prompt exceeds the maximum size for a <code>Task</code> call",
"The subagent will refuse to answer a narrow question given broad context",
"Large prompts always produce worse answers regardless of task"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Delegation scope should match task scope; excess context costs money and encourages scope creep.",
"No such limit is the issue here; the prompt is well within normal bounds.",
"It will not refuse &mdash; it will answer, possibly with unrequested extras.",
"Too absolute. Large context is exactly right for a document-analysis task; the mismatch is what is wrong here."],
e:"Note the symmetry with the earlier question about passing a full document for analysis. Both are about <em>matching</em> context to task, not about minimising or maximising it. Analysis of a 12,000-word document needs the document. Verifying a single date needs the claim, the source, and the criterion for confirmation. Over-scoping a narrow delegation wastes tokens and produces answers padded with material nobody asked for, which then costs the coordinator context to absorb.",
k:"Match delegation scope to task scope. Over-scoping wastes tokens and invites scope creep; under-scoping starves the task."},

{i:154,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent calls <code>get_customer</code> and the tool returns three matching records for the name provided.",
ask:"What should the agent do?",
a:["Ask the customer for an additional identifier such as an order number, email or postcode, and re-query",
"Pick the record with the most recent activity, as it is most likely the right one",
"Proceed with all three and let downstream tools filter",
"Escalate immediately to a human"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Multiple matches are a genuine ambiguity that additional identifying information resolves cleanly.",
"A heuristic guess on identity, and acting on the wrong customer's account is exactly the harm the verification step exists to prevent.",
"Carrying three candidate identities forward makes an eventual wrong action more likely, not less.",
"Premature. The agent has an obvious, low-friction way to resolve this within its own scope."],
e:"The blueprint states it directly: multiple customer matches require asking for additional identifiers, not heuristic guessing. This also illustrates the boundary between ambiguity and escalation &mdash; escalate when policy is silent or the customer asks for a human, but resolve ambiguity yourself when a simple clarifying question will do it. Asking for one more identifier is fast, safe, and preserves first-contact resolution.",
k:"Multiple matches &rarr; ask for another identifier and re-query. Never guess identity heuristically; do not escalate what a question resolves."},

{i:155,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You want to prevent an agent from ever sending an email to an address outside your organisation's domain.",
ask:"Which implementation is correct?",
a:["A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that inspects the recipient argument and blocks the call when the domain does not match the allow-list",
"A tool description stating that only internal recipients are permitted",
"A system prompt rule listing the permitted domain",
"A post-send audit that flags external recipients for review"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Inspecting arguments before execution is exactly what <code>PreToolUse</code> is for, and it makes the rule unbypassable.",
"Descriptions inform selection; they cannot inspect or block an argument value.",
"A prompt rule is guidance and will hold most of the time, which is not good enough for outbound communication.",
"Detects the leak after the email has left, which for an outbound action is too late."],
e:"Outbound communication is irreversible and externally visible, so it belongs firmly in the enforcement category. A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook can inspect the arguments the model produced and refuse. Two details matter: the block message must explain the rule and be marked non-retryable so the agent does not loop, and the check should be a positive allow-list rather than a deny-list, since enumerating forbidden domains can never be complete.",
k:"Argument-level restrictions go in <code>PreToolUse</code>. Prefer allow-lists to deny-lists, and return a clear non-retryable block message."},

{i:156,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"CCI",diff:2,
q:"A code review agent is given a 3,000-line diff in a single prompt. Its feedback is generic and focuses almost entirely on the first few hundred lines.",
ask:"What is happening and what is the fix?",
a:["Attention dilutes across a large input; split into per-file passes and add a separate cross-file integration pass",
"The model ran out of output tokens; increase <code>max_tokens</code>",
"The diff format is unparseable; convert it to full file contents",
"The review criteria are too strict; relax them"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Concentrated attention on the early part of a long input is the signature of attention dilution, and multi-pass review is the named remedy.",
"Output limits would truncate the response mid-sentence, not produce complete but shallow feedback on the first section.",
"Diffs are perfectly parseable; converting to full files would make the input even larger.",
"Strictness governs what gets reported, not which parts of the input are examined."],
e:"Recognise the symptom: complete-looking output that is disproportionately concentrated on the beginning of a long input. This is the lost-in-the-middle effect from the reviewer's side. The fix is structural &mdash; per-file passes so each file gets full attention, plus a separate cross-file pass over the changed symbols and their call sites to catch integration defects no per-file pass can see.",
k:"Shallow feedback weighted to the start of a long input = attention dilution. Split into per-file passes + a cross-file integration pass."},

{i:157,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"You want to preserve the reasoning behind an architectural decision so that a future session &mdash; or a colleague &mdash; can pick up the work without re-deriving it.",
ask:"What is the most effective practice?",
a:["Write the decision, the alternatives considered and the rationale to a file in the repository",
"Rely on the session transcript, which can be resumed later",
"Ask Claude to remember the decision for future sessions",
"Put the rationale in a code comment at the site of the change"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A durable file survives compaction, crashes, session expiry and personnel changes, and is reviewable and version-controlled.",
"Transcripts are long, personal and lossy under compaction, and a colleague cannot resume your session.",
"There is no cross-session memory to rely on; each session starts from its configured context.",
"Useful for local context, but a comment at one site cannot carry a decision that spans several modules, nor the alternatives rejected."],
e:"Anything that must survive a session boundary belongs in a file. This is the same reasoning as the scratchpad pattern for findings, applied to decisions: write what was decided, what alternatives were considered, and why the chosen one won. Committed to the repository it becomes reviewable, discoverable and available as context to any future session &mdash; and if it encodes a durable convention rather than a one-off decision, CLAUDE.md or a rules file is its natural home.",
k:"Durable knowledge goes in files, not transcripts. Decisions, rationale and rejected alternatives survive sessions; conversations do not."},

{i:158,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"CAP",diff:2,
q:"A user asks a follow-up question that depends on a calculation the agent performed twelve turns earlier. The agent recomputes it and gets a slightly different answer.",
ask:"Which practice prevents this?",
a:["Persist computed results as explicit facts in a durable state block rather than relying on the model to recall or recompute them",
"Instruct the model to always show its working so it can check itself",
"Reduce temperature to zero so recomputation is deterministic",
"Ask the user to repeat the figures from the earlier answer"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A computed value is a fact; store it once and re-inject it rather than recomputing from a degraded context.",
"Visible working helps a reader spot an error but does not guarantee the same result twice.",
"Temperature zero reduces sampling variance but does not guarantee identical reasoning from a context that has since changed.",
"Pushes the system's memory problem onto the user."],
e:"Derived values deserve the same treatment as retrieved facts: compute once, store explicitly, re-inject. The case-facts block is the natural home &mdash; verified identity, order in scope, quoted amounts, computed totals, decisions made. Recomputation from a long, partially-degraded context is exactly where small inconsistencies creep in, and inconsistent numbers destroy user trust faster than almost any other failure.",
k:"Compute once, store as an explicit fact, re-inject. Never rely on recomputation from a long context for consistency."},

{i:159,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Your multi-agent system works well in testing but in production one subagent occasionally takes 10x longer than expected, and the coordinator has no timeout.",
ask:"What should be added, and what must accompany it?",
a:["A per-delegation timeout, plus a defined behaviour for what the coordinator does when it fires &mdash; retry, route elsewhere, or proceed with annotated partial coverage",
"A per-delegation timeout alone, cancelling the workflow when it fires",
"A longer overall workflow timeout so slow subagents can finish",
"An instruction in the subagent's prompt telling it to work faster"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A timeout without a defined response just converts a hang into an abort; the recovery policy is the substantive half.",
"Cancelling the whole workflow because one optional subtask was slow discards everything that succeeded.",
"Postpones the hang without bounding it, and gives no recovery path.",
"A prompt cannot control the latency of tool calls and external services."],
e:"Timeouts are only half of a resilience design. The other half is the policy: is this delegation on the critical path or optional? Does a timeout trigger a retry (only if the failure is plausibly transient), a route to an alternative agent, or delivery with annotated partial coverage? Deciding this in advance is what turns an incident into a graceful degradation, and it connects directly to structured error propagation and coverage annotation.",
k:"Every timeout needs a paired recovery policy: retry, reroute, or proceed with annotated partial coverage. A bare timeout is just a tidier hang."},

{i:160,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Which of these is the clearest example of a case where prompt-based guidance is genuinely insufficient?",
ask:"Which case requires programmatic enforcement?",
a:["A regulated workflow where a specific verification step must precede a financial transaction in 100% of cases",
"A preference for concise responses over verbose ones",
"A convention that variable names should use snake_case",
"A guideline that the agent should acknowledge the customer's issue before proposing a solution"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A 100% requirement on a regulated financial control is precisely where probabilistic compliance fails an audit.",
"A style preference; occasional verbosity costs nothing that matters.",
"A code convention, appropriately handled by CLAUDE.md or a rules file, and caught by linting if it matters.",
"A conversational quality guideline where occasional deviation is tolerable."],
e:"The test to apply is: what is the cost of the single violation in a hundred? For style, tone and conventions, the answer is &ldquo;negligible&rdquo;, so prompts are right and preserve flexibility. For regulated financial controls the answer is &ldquo;an audit finding, a refund to the wrong party, or a compliance breach&rdquo;, so it must be a precondition or hook. Note also that conventions have a third home &mdash; deterministic tooling like linters and formatters &mdash; which is better than either prompts or hooks for mechanical rules.",
k:"Ask what one violation in a hundred costs. Negligible &rarr; prompt. Audit finding &rarr; code. Mechanical &rarr; linter."},

{i:161,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"An extraction workflow currently runs: extract &rarr; validate &rarr; persist. You want to add human review for low-confidence extractions without slowing the high-confidence path.",
ask:"How should the pipeline change?",
a:["Add a routing step after validation that sends low-confidence or ambiguous extractions to a review queue while the rest persist directly",
"Send every extraction to human review to be safe",
"Ask the model to decide whether each extraction needs review",
"Review a random 10% sample and persist everything else"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Route on measurable signals so friction lands only where it is needed, keeping the fast path fast.",
"Uniform review defeats the automation entirely and produces reviewer fatigue.",
"Self-assessed need for review is uncalibrated, and it lets the model authorise skipping its own review.",
"Random sampling measures quality &mdash; genuinely valuable &mdash; but it does not catch the specific low-confidence items that need attention."],
e:"Confidence-based routing is the blueprint's human-oversight pattern, with an important caveat: the confidence must be calibrated against a labelled validation set before you trust a threshold, ideally at field level rather than document level. Note that D and the correct answer are complementary rather than competing &mdash; stratified random sampling of the <em>high</em>-confidence stream is how you detect new error patterns that the confidence signal is missing.",
k:"Route low-confidence items to review; sample the high-confidence stream to detect drift. Calibrate thresholds on labelled data first."},

{i:162,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"You spawn a subagent to answer a question and it returns an answer with no file paths, no line references and no quotes &mdash; just a confident summary.",
ask:"What should you suspect first?",
a:["That it may lack the tools to actually inspect the code, and is answering from general knowledge",
"That the answer is correct but the subagent chose a terse style",
"That the coordinator's prompt was too long",
"That the model temperature was set too low"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Ungrounded confidence is the signature of an agent with no tool access or one that failed to use its tools.",
"Possible, but the safe first hypothesis is that the answer is not grounded &mdash; that is the failure mode with real consequences.",
"Prompt length does not remove citations from an answer.",
"Temperature has no bearing on whether the answer is grounded in inspected files."],
e:"A model with no tools does not error &mdash; it answers from priors, fluently. Two defences: validate at spawn time that the agent has the tools its role requires, and make the output contract demand citable evidence (file paths, line numbers, quotes, URLs). An answer that cannot cite anything specific should be treated as unverified regardless of how confident it sounds. This is the same discipline as requiring claim-to-source mappings from research subagents.",
k:"No citations = possibly no tools. Validate tool grants at spawn and require citable evidence in every output contract."},

{i:163,d:1,t:"1.5",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your <code>PreToolUse</code> hook needs to check an external policy service before allowing a refund. The policy service is down.",
ask:"What should the hook do?",
a:["Fail closed &mdash; deny the refund and return a clear non-retryable explanation directing the agent to escalate",
"Fail open &mdash; allow the refund, since blocking would harm the customer experience",
"Retry the policy service indefinitely until it responds",
"Skip the check and log a warning for later review"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. For a financial control, an unverifiable policy state must be treated as not-approved, with a graceful escalation path.",
"Failing open on a financial control means issuing unapproved refunds during exactly the incident when oversight is weakest.",
"An unbounded retry converts a fast failure into a hang, and the customer waits with no outcome.",
"Skipping the check and logging is failing open with paperwork &mdash; the refund still goes out unapproved."],
e:"Enforcement hooks fail closed; observational hooks fail open. When a control cannot be evaluated, the safe interpretation of an unknown policy state is &ldquo;not approved&rdquo;. The design that makes this acceptable is the escalation path: the customer is not simply refused, they are routed to a human who can approve manually. That combination &mdash; deny plus a clear, non-retryable explanation plus a route to a person &mdash; keeps the control intact without stranding the customer.",
k:"Enforcement hooks fail closed with a clear escalation path. Observational hooks fail open. Decide this per hook, in advance."},

{i:164,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You want to compare how two different system prompts affect an agent's behaviour on the same 50 test cases.",
ask:"Which setup gives a valid comparison?",
a:["Run both variants against identical inputs in independent sessions, so no shared context can influence either",
"Run variant A, then variant B in the same session so the model can compare them",
"Fork a session after running variant A and run variant B in the fork",
"Run both variants and ask the model which prompt it preferred"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Independent runs on identical inputs is the only configuration that isolates the variable you are testing.",
"The second run sees the first in context, so it is not measuring the prompt &mdash; it is measuring the prompt plus contamination.",
"The fork inherits everything before it, including variant A's outputs, so B is not evaluated cleanly.",
"Model self-preference is not a measurement of behaviour on the test cases."],
e:"Evaluation demands independence for the same reason review does: shared context contaminates. Hold the inputs constant, vary one thing, run in isolated contexts, and score against a fixed rubric or labelled expectations. This is the same principle behind using an independent instance to review generated code, and behind the warning that an agent asked to critique its own work retains the reasoning that produced it.",
k:"Valid comparison = identical inputs, one variable, independent contexts, fixed scoring. Shared context invalidates the measurement."},

{i:165,d:1,t:"1.2",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your report-generation agent receives synthesised findings and must render them. The findings include financial tables, news summaries and technical benchmark results.",
ask:"What should govern how each is rendered?",
a:["Render by content type &mdash; financial data as tables, news as prose, technical findings as structured lists",
"Render everything as prose for a consistent reading experience",
"Render everything as bullet points for scannability",
"Let the model choose a format freely for each section"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Matching presentation to content type is called out explicitly, and it is what makes each kind of information usable.",
"Financial figures in prose are hard to compare and easy to misread &mdash; a table exists for a reason.",
"Bullet points flatten tabular relationships and make narrative context choppy.",
"Free choice produces inconsistency across runs and sections, and undermines the report's scannability."],
e:"A small but explicitly tested point: render by content type. Financial and quantitative data belong in tables where values align and can be compared; narrative and contextual material reads better as prose; technical findings and enumerable results suit structured lists. Consistency matters too &mdash; the same content type should render the same way every time, so readers learn where to look.",
k:"Render by content type: tables for quantitative data, prose for narrative, structured lists for technical findings. Be consistent across runs."},

{i:166,d:1,t:"1.1",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You need Claude to always produce a structured result on the first turn of a workflow, then converse normally afterwards.",
ask:"How should <code>tool_choice</code> be configured?",
a:["Force the specific extraction tool on the first turn, then switch to <code>auto</code> for subsequent turns",
"Use <code>any</code> for every turn of the workflow",
"Use <code>auto</code> throughout and rely on the prompt to request structure",
"Use <code>none</code> on the first turn and <code>auto</code> afterwards"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Forcing the tool guarantees the structured first result, and switching to <code>auto</code> lets the conversation terminate naturally.",
"<code>any</code> throughout means the model can never emit a text answer, so the workflow can never end.",
"<code>auto</code> permits a text response on turn one, which is exactly the guarantee you needed.",
"<code>none</code> forbids tool calls entirely, so no structured output is possible on turn one."],
e:"<code>tool_choice</code> is per-request, so vary it across the loop. The common pattern is a constrained first turn &mdash; <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract\"}</code> to force a specific schema, or <code>any</code> when several schemas are valid &mdash; followed by <code>auto</code> for the conversational remainder. Know the fourth value too: <code>none</code> forbids tool use, which is useful when you want a purely textual summarisation turn.",
k:"<code>tool_choice</code> is per-request. Force a tool on turn one for guaranteed structure, then <code>auto</code>. <code>none</code> forbids tools entirely."},

{i:167,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"After escalation, a human agent resolves the case. The customer returns two days later with a related question and the AI agent has no idea what the human decided.",
ask:"What is missing?",
a:["A write-back path so the human's resolution and reasoning are recorded in case state the agent reads on the next contact",
"A longer session timeout so the original session is still resumable",
"A rule that escalated customers must always reach a human on future contacts",
"A larger context window so the agent can hold more history"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Escalation must be a round trip: the handoff goes out, and the resolution comes back into durable case state.",
"Sessions are the wrong storage for facts that must outlive them and be visible to other channels.",
"Permanently routing a customer to humans after one escalation is expensive and unnecessary.",
"Window size cannot hold information that was never written down anywhere."],
e:"Escalation designs usually get the outbound half right &mdash; a structured handoff with id, issue, what was tried and a recommendation &mdash; and forget the return path. Without a write-back, every post-escalation contact starts blind, the customer repeats themselves, and the agent may contradict what the human decided. Persist the resolution, the reasoning and any exception granted into case state that the agent reads on the next contact.",
k:"Escalation is a round trip. Structured handoff out, resolution and reasoning written back into durable case state."},

{i:168,d:1,t:"1.6",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You are decomposing a task and must choose between decomposing by <em>phase</em> (research, then design, then implement) and by <em>component</em> (auth, then billing, then reporting).",
ask:"What should drive the choice?",
a:["Where the dependencies actually lie &mdash; decompose along the axis that minimises cross-subtask coupling",
"Always decompose by phase, since it matches how projects are managed",
"Always decompose by component, since components map to code structure",
"Whichever produces the larger number of subtasks"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The goal of decomposition is to produce subtasks that can proceed with minimal cross-talk, and that means cutting where the coupling is weakest.",
"Phase decomposition is right when each phase needs the previous phase's output across the whole system, but it forces full serialisation.",
"Component decomposition is right when components are genuinely independent, and wrong when they share contracts that must be designed together.",
"Subtask count is not a goal; over-decomposition is a named risk."],
e:"Decomposition quality is measured by coupling. Cut along the axis where subtasks share the least, so each can proceed with its own context and the results compose cleanly. Highly coupled components with shared contracts argue for phase decomposition, so the contract is designed once. Genuinely independent components argue for component decomposition, which additionally unlocks parallelism. Hybrids are common and legitimate: a shared design phase, then parallel per-component implementation.",
k:"Decompose along the axis of weakest coupling. Coupled components &rarr; by phase. Independent components &rarr; by component (and in parallel)."},

{i:169,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A subagent is given <code>allowedTools: [\"Read\", \"Grep\", \"Glob\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Bash\", \"WebFetch\", \"Task\"]</code> for a task that only requires reading and searching code.",
ask:"What are the two problems?",
a:["It violates least privilege by granting write, shell and network capability, and the oversized toolset degrades selection reliability",
"The tool list exceeds the maximum length permitted in an <code>AgentDefinition</code>",
"<code>Grep</code> and <code>Glob</code> are redundant and one should be removed",
"<code>Task</code> is required for all subagents and the rest are optional"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Both the security problem and the reliability problem follow from the same over-grant.",
"Length is not the issue; the inappropriate capabilities are.",
"<code>Grep</code> and <code>Glob</code> do different jobs &mdash; content search versus filename matching &mdash; and both are appropriate here.",
"Backwards: <code>Task</code> belongs on coordinators, and giving it to a subagent lets it spawn children and flatten your trace."],
e:"Two independent arguments converge on the same fix. Security: a read-only task granted <code>Write</code>, <code>Edit</code> and <code>Bash</code> can modify the repository and execute arbitrary commands, and <code>WebFetch</code> can exfiltrate. Reliability: selection accuracy falls as the toolset grows. Granting <code>Task</code> to a subagent is a third, subtler error &mdash; it lets spokes spawn their own spokes, producing a mesh with no single observable trace.",
k:"Over-granting tools is both a security and a reliability defect. Keep <code>Task</code> on coordinators only, or your hub-and-spoke becomes a mesh."},

{i:170,d:1,t:"1.7",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"Halfway through a complex multi-phase task, you realise the approach chosen in phase one was wrong.",
ask:"What is the best recovery?",
a:["Return to the plan, revise it with what you now know, and restart execution from the phase where the approach diverged",
"Continue to the end and fix everything afterwards",
"Start the entire task over from scratch in a new session",
"Ask the agent to patch around the wrong approach in later phases"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A written plan makes targeted revision possible: change the decision, keep the phases that are still valid, re-execute from the divergence.",
"Compounds the error across every remaining phase and makes the eventual fix far larger.",
"Discards phases that were correct and independent of the bad decision &mdash; more expensive than necessary.",
"Patching around a wrong foundation accumulates complexity and leaves the original defect in place."],
e:"This is one of the concrete payoffs of writing the plan to a file before executing. With a plan you can identify exactly which phase embodied the wrong decision, which downstream phases depended on it, and which are independent and can be kept. Without one, you are left choosing between carrying on regardless and starting over. Update the plan with the revised decision and the reasoning, then re-execute from that point.",
k:"A written plan makes partial rollback possible: revise the decision, keep independent phases, re-execute from the divergence point."}
);
